From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Subject: [PATCH] dqevent: fall back to the 120-byte struct on pre-4.0 kernels

The old Halium device kernels that were forward-ported to build v4l2loopback
(the HP TouchPad's 3.4 tree) define struct v4l2_event with a 4-byte-aligned
union: it has no __s64 member there, so the struct is 120 bytes. glibc's modern
uapi pads the union to 8 bytes (v4l2_event_ctrl carries __s64), making the
struct 128 bytes - so VIDIOC_DQEVENT encodes a different _IOC_SIZE and the old
kernel does not recognise it, returning EINVAL. VIDIOC_SUBSCRIBE_EVENT is
unaffected (its argument has no timespec/union), which is why subscribing works
but dequeuing fails.

Issue the dequeue with the kernel's 120-byte layout as a fallback, only after
the standard call fails with EINVAL. type and the event payload sit at the same
offsets in both layouts, so the client-usage handling below is unchanged. On
4.0+ kernels the 128-byte struct matches and the fallback never runs.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [LuneOS support for forward-ported pre-4.0 kernels]
---
 src/droidcam2v4l2.c | 34 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 34 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/droidcam2v4l2.c b/src/droidcam2v4l2.c
index 0000000..0000001 100644
--- a/src/droidcam2v4l2.c
+++ b/src/droidcam2v4l2.c
@@ -558,6 +558,28 @@ stop_preview(camera_config *conf)
     pthread_mutex_unlock(&camera_lock);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Pre-4.0 kernels forward-ported for v4l2loopback (e.g. the HP TouchPad's 3.4
+ * tree) define struct v4l2_event with a 4-byte-aligned union - it has no __s64
+ * member there - so the struct is 120 bytes. glibc's modern uapi pads the union
+ * to 8 bytes (it carries __s64 in v4l2_event_ctrl), making the struct 128 bytes
+ * and VIDIOC_DQEVENT a different ioctl number that such kernels reject (EINVAL).
+ * This is the kernel's 120-byte layout and matching dequeue request; it is used
+ * only as a fallback when the standard call fails with EINVAL, so modern (4.0+)
+ * kernels, where the 128-byte struct matches, are unaffected.
+ */
+struct v4l2_event_k120 {
+	__u32 type;
+	union {
+		__u8 data[64];
+	} u;
+	__u32 pending;
+	__u32 sequence;
+	struct timespec timestamp;
+	__u32 reserved[9];
+};
+#define VIDIOC_DQEVENT_K120 _IOR('V', 89, struct v4l2_event_k120)
+
 void*
 camera_event_loop(void *userdata)
 {
@@ -570,6 +592,18 @@ camera_event_loop(void *userdata)
         struct v4l2_event event;
         g_debug("[CAMERA %d] Waiting for events...", conf->v4l2_idx);
         int ret = ioctl(conf->v4l2_fd, VIDIOC_DQEVENT, &event);
+        if (ret < 0 && errno == EINVAL) {
+            /* Old (pre-4.0) kernel with the 120-byte struct v4l2_event: retry
+             * with its layout. type and u sit at the same offsets, so the
+             * client-usage payload below still reads correctly. */
+            struct v4l2_event_k120 ek;
+            ret = ioctl(conf->v4l2_fd, VIDIOC_DQEVENT_K120, &ek);
+            if (ret >= 0) {
+                memset(&event, 0, sizeof(event));
+                event.type = ek.type;
+                memcpy(event.u.data, ek.u.data, sizeof(ek.u.data));
+            }
+        }
         if (ret < 0) {
             if (errno == EBADF) {
                 if (!conf->asleep)
